*ρ(Q) = 0.8632924...**\n- First hitting time bound: **Pr(T_{I₂} > N) ≤ 27 · ρ(Q)^N ≤ 27 · 2^{-0.2121·N}**\n- Expected hitting time ≤ 10.142 steps from worst transient state\n- Half-life ≈ 4.72 Collatz steps\n\n**(iv) Stationary visit rate** (Theorem 16635-16647, lines 16635-16647):  \n- Exact stationary mass: **π(I₂) = 10121/65280 ≈ 0.15504** (99.23% of uniform density 5/32)\n- Cycle-level contraction argument: mean log₂-drop per step μ = **0.4185 bits/step** (agrees with spectral rate α_cyl = 0.4152 to 0.8%)\n- Mean cascade-gap cycle length: 6.45 steps; mean valuation per cycle: 12.92 bits\n\n**Mechanism of unconditional result:**  \nLines 16543-16559 explain why this avoids circularity:\n- Distributional input required: cylinder distribution within I₂ is uniform\n- This follows from algebraic fact gcd(3^k,64) = 1 (odd-slope unit property)\n- Does NOT require cascade-exit iterate to be uniform mod 2^{S+R}\n- Does NOT require Pr(ℰ_R) small a priori\n- Spectral contraction is an **independent** decay source running parallel to (not through) the spectator-bit loop\n\n---\n\n### 2. **\"Ruffini reduction\" of the depth-2 known-gap kernel**\n\n**Content** (lines 16435-16484):\n\nThe Ruffini collapse (Proposition 16445) establishes via synthetic division that:\n- The characteristic polynomial **factors exactly as λ⁴(λ − 129/1024)**\n- Proof uses trace identities: tr(B̃₂^k) = (129/1024)^k for k=1,2,3 to pin down the nilpotent block\n- The Jordan form shows: nilpotent block size ≤ 4 but vanishes by k=2 (rank collapse to 1)\n- All non-zero singular structure concentrated on the q≡7 residue class row\n\n**Interpretation** (lines 16472-16484):  \nThe known-gap component is already spectrally exhausted. The obstruction to full convergence proof does NOT live in B̃₂ itself; it lives in the unresolved **gap-≥6 channels** that Proposition 16 (pair-return) omits. The natural next step is enlarging B̃₂ to include gap-≥6 and asking whether the bound survives (which Theorem 16498 addresses: yes, it survives at ρ ≤ 5/32).\n\n---\n\n### 3. **\"Transient-prefix exponential tail\" and \"Stationary visit rate\": Statements & Constants**\n\nSee section 1(iii) and 1(iv) above. Key numbers:\n\n| Quantity | Value | Line |\n|----------|-------|------|\n| **ρ(Q)** (transient Perron root) | 0.8632924... | 16595 |\n| **Exponential rate** | 2^{-0.2121·N} | 16601 |\n| **Expected hitting time** | 10.142 steps | 16604 |\n| **Half-life** | 4.72 steps | 16606 |\n| **π(I₂)** (exact) | 10121/65280 | 16642 |\n| **Mean log-drop** | 0.4185 bits/step | 16666 |\n| **Mean cycle length** | 6.45 steps | 16671 |\n\n**Status**: All four results are **rigorous and unconditional**. They do not assume the Collatz conjecture or any unproven hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **The \"(C-2adic) bound on BF residue classes\"**\n\n**What are BF residue classes?** (lines 17196-17206)  \n- BF = \"Burst-Fold\" / \"Karp pattern $[1,1,1,3]$\"\n- B_k := {n ∈ ℕ : n ≡ n_k^* (mod 2^{6k+8})} where n_k^* is the BF k-champion (smallest integer matching k laps of the pattern $[1,1,1,3]$ before escaping)\n- Each B_k is a unique residue class mod 2^{6k+8} under the Hensel lift (Theorem 17 line 17106-17107)\n\n**The bound** (Theorem 17208-17224, lines 17208-17224):\n\nFor ℵ-almost every n ∈ B_k ∩ ℕ (where ℵ is Haar measure on ℤ₂):\n$$\\tau(n) \\le C \\cdot (k + \\log_2 n)$$\nwith **C independent of k**, derived from:\n- Ergodic theorem applied to the additive cocycle log_2(T_2(n)/n)\n- Expected log-growth = log₂3 − 2 ≈ −0.415 bits (strictly negative)\n- Birkhoff ergodic theorem on a Haar-null exceptional set\n\n**Empirical witness form** (Corollary 17252-17272, lines 17252-17272):\n- 39,140 uniformly random samples, k ∈ [1,14], log₂n ∈ [20,171]\n- **C ≤ 5.04** for k ≥ 9 (14,400 samples)\n- **C ≤ 6.49** for k ≥ 4, log₂n ≤ 106 (15,000 samples)\n- **C ≤ 9.87** unconditionally (39,140 total, one k=2 outlier)\n- Empirical mean C ≈ 2.78 ± 0.05, flat in k and log₂n\n- Crandall-normalized τ/log₂n ≈ 3.0, well belo